    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by the whole number.\n\nContent:\nFor example, to calculate [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2},] we multiply the fraction's denominator by [MATH: {\\color{blue}2}\\!:]\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{5} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = \\dfrac{1}{5 \\times {\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{1}{10}]\n\nContent:\nNotice that we get the same answer using a fraction model, and the process is similar.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/5ddb7251d1e11abbce9782ed282533e3]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Dividing a Unit Fraction\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3 ?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{4} \\div 3 = \\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{1}{12}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(3) \u00f7 2 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(6)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(32)]\nc. [MATH: (3)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(3) \u00f7 20 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (20)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(30)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(45)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(60)]\ne. [MATH: (3)/(20)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(4) \u00f7 22 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Dividing a Unit Fraction With a Two-Digit Denominator\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5 ?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{16} \\div 5 = \\dfrac{1}{16 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{80}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(11) \u00f7 3 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (11)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(33)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(14)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(41)]\ne. [MATH: (3)/(11)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(25) \u00f7 3 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Word Problems\n\nExample Prompt:\nJoshua uses [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{kg}] of ham to make [MATH: 4] pizzas. If the ham is distributed equally among the [MATH: 4] pizzas, how much ham does he put on each pizza?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo determine the amount of ham on each pizza, we must divide the amount of ham by the number of pizzas:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4]\n\nTo divide a unit fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's denominator by that whole number:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}]\n\nTherefore, Joshua puts [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{8} \\, \\textrm{kg}] of ham on each pizza.\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nJohn has [MATH: (1)/(4)] gallons of paint to paint [MATH: 2] doors. If he uses the same amount of paint on each door, how much paint will John use on each door?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(14)] gallons\nb. [MATH: (1)/(18)] gallons\nc. [MATH: (1)/(8)] gallons\nd. [MATH: (1)/(6)] gallons\ne. [MATH: (1)/(16)] gallons\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\nElizabeth has [MATH: (1)/(2)] of a cake to distribute among [MATH: 6] children. If each child gets the same size piece, how much of the cake will each child get?Expressed as a fraction, each child will get\n[FREE_ENTRY 1] of the cake."
